  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 306 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n(Wednesday - Wednesday Night)\n\nScattered showers and (elevated) thunderstorms are expected in \nsoutheast Missouri Wednesday morning as a midlevel shortwave trough \nmoves across the area with weak low-level moisture convergence. This \nactivity should move south with time. While the low-level jet \nabates, the midlevel wave may be strong enough to keep convection \ngoing to near or just past midday. If that indeed happens, the \nstorms could become more surface based and pose a damaging wind \nthreat before they exit. There is low confidence in this happening \nhowever.\n\nA slow-moving cold front is expected to move through the area \nWednesday afternoon through the early overnight hours as a longwave \ntrough deepens across the Great Lakes. Large scale forcing for \nascent is not particularly strong during this time period, though \nthere is some weak diffluence aloft along with some cooling. Surface \nconvergence is also on the weak side along the boundary itself, but \noverall lift is expected to be strong enough to overcome some \ncapping (CINH 50-100 J/kg) in place. The coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms however may stay isolated to widely scattered right \nnear the front itself. The environment does not look particularly \nfavorable for organized convection, but there may be just enough \ninstability in the presence of moderately strong (~30 knots) deep-\nlayer shear to produce a couple of strong-severe storms capable of \nproducing 60 mph winds. The latest HREF shows some pockets of 40-\n50+% probabilities for at least 25 knots of shear and SBCAPE of \n1000+ J/kg near the boundary. \n\n\n(Thursday - Saturday Night)\n\nA period of near to below normal temperatures along with dry \nconditions is forecast to end the week and start the weekend as a \nsurface ridge of high pressure settles into the mid-Mississippi \nValley. The coolest period of time is expected to be between \nThursday night and Friday night. Lows each night are expected to be \nin the low to mid 60s each night, with some spots quite possibly in \nthe upper 50s. Highs on Friday should predominantly be in the mid \n80s.\n\n\n(Sunday - Monday)\n\nAll signs to a fast warmup to end the weekend into early next week \nas the mid/upper level ridge amplifies and moves into the \nMississippi Valley. The air mass looks pretty warm with 850-hPa \ntemperatures likely above +20C (>90th percentile). Surface dewpoints \n(relative humidity) levels also look to climb by early next week \ninto the low 70s (75th-90th percentile). High temperatures well into \nthe 90s seem quite plausible with heat index values potentially in \nthe 100-105F range at least for portions of the area. In other \nwords, while the calendar will turn to September next week, summer \nmay not be giving up its grip. There is more confidence in \nincreasing heat in southern sections of the area at this point in \ntime due to the likely position of the ridge across the mid south. \nAcross northeast Missouri and west-central Illinois, the spread in \nhigh temperatures is higher due to at least some potential for a \nfrontal boundary to be in the area as well as \"ridge-runners\"/waves \nof showers and thunderstorms.",
